00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.540 align:start position:0%
 
it's great to be joined today by Ron fry  a green beret warrior king of the pêche  Valley entrepreneur and author of the  book hammerhead six how Green Berets  waged an unconventional war against the  Taliban to win in Afghanistan's deadly  pêche Valley the book is fascinating Ron  and it's such a pleasure to be speaking  with you today you know I think when  most people learn about the tactics and  strategies employed by the American  military in the Middle East they they  hear more about strategies that are not  what you were involved in when you were  in the pêche Valley so talk to us a  little bit about your deployment there  and and what you did great well hey  thanks first of all thanks for having me  on pleasure you know right after the  Taliban fell in 2001-2002 timeframe you  know there's this big power vacuum and  we're trying to help this this country  get on its feet they're still like you  know pockets of al Qaeda and Taliban  that are in the area when my team got  deployed there we were in the the Kunar  province which you know the history of  the war would show was the hottest part  of the of the fight or the most  difficult place to fight and we were  just trying to figure it out there was  only a few boots on the ground and we  just got this unique mission that  there's this valley where they knew al  Qaeda was operating they knew that  there's Taliban operatives there was all  kinds of cartels and other people in  this valley and we got the we got the  job to go into the valley and attempt to  win the people over and deny the enemy's  sanctuary in that valley and that's the  essence of what our mission was and so  we had to you know like you alluded to  we couldn't take the heavy-handed  approach of going in there with a  thousand men and trying to force these  people to either work with us or submit  to us we actually had to go out there  and win them over and you know people  talk about hearts and minds but we  actually had to go there and make  friends and recruit soldiers from the  local people to actually work with us  can you talk a little bit about some of  the sort of day to day maybe even some  of the mundane stuff that you had to  sort of learn and integrate and and live  within in or  to start to be accepted into the  community yes you know the the huge  advantage we had is when we showed up  all the American soldiers they'd seen  before were clean-shaven young uniformed  soldiers and all of a sudden you have  these older Americans that have beards  that are kind of wearing some local  clothes that are willing to talk to the  shopkeeper that are buying you know our  Pepsi's and stuff on the local economy  so immediately when we got there there  is about a two-week honeymoon period  where they thought these new Americans  were Muslim they kind of accepted us  immediately and were like okay these  guys are different and then you know  that kind of wore off and and then it  was like we actually had to do our job  but you know on a day-to-day basis I was  trying to run a camp so I've got some  Marines that are working with us my team  some Afghans they weren't mercenaries  but they were working for us and you  know we had to deal with the day-to-day  where you had people bringing their kids  up that needed medical care we had  people working on the camp doing labor  we had Afghan soldiers that we were  working with that we were training and  then probably five or six times a day I  would get called down to the local  district leaders house and either you  know adjudicate some dispute between  villages or make a decision with a  contractor it was like we were running  almost like the mayor's office of this  of this valley and we're supposed to be  you know the the strength and the I was  considered like the local warlord but at  the same time I was I was their guest  and I was kind of helping them with  their local politics where some of the  disputes they had they couldn't they  couldn't fix themselves they needed a  third party to come in and basically be  the referee what was the language  situation how was the communication  happening on a day to day basis so all  Special Forces teams have language  skills unfortunate thing my team was an  Asian team so we had like eight  different Asian languages but nobody  spoke posh tune and so we had a couple  of key interpreters that had been vetted  by the CIA before and they just would  help us with with all the translating  can you sort of relate or maybe give us  an analogy to so for people who don't  know your background you went to Brigham  Young you spent two years  a missionary for the Mormon Church in  France and Switzerland yes can you talk  a little bit about how well number one  how that experience related directly to  your work in the pest valley but also  maybe ways in which it was drastically  different what you were doing there and  what you were doing as a Mormon  missionary yes that's a great question  you know as a Mormon missionary in  Switzerland  you know most missionaries in other  parts of the world they do a lot of  humanitarian aid both my brothers serve  their Muslims in Chile and about half  the time they were doing more Peace  Corps type stuff as opposed to direct  service for the church in Switzerland in  France I got to meet a ton of Muslims  people from every different faith and we  got to do service we also got to  proselyting but I got a real  understanding of the world is a big  place there's a lot of different  ideologies a lot of different people a  lot of different thoughts and spend a  lot of time listening to people hearing  their life stories hearing what was  driving them and I felt when we were in  Afghanistan that skill set was directly  transferable in the fact that these  people want to be heard they want to  know that whatever decision you're  making or for whatever reason you're  there that you understand where they're  coming from whether that's what they  want their government to do what they  don't want the US military to do you  know the goals they have for their kids  etc the ability to listen and actually  take their point of view into account  was directly transferable what wasn't  was you do that during the day and then  at night when you get Intel that the  enemy is doing something another place  and then you strap on the guns and go  they're looking for a fight that was  that was not directly transferable the  people skills the that was transferable  but that was really the difference you  know sometimes I felt like I was more  like a missionary during the day and  then when you know bullets would start  flying then you switch right back into  Green Beret or army mode when you see  the discussions that happen currently in  media among elected officials that  relate to whether it's Isis or really  and any other group or area where there  could be a potential military incursion  by the United States or a joint group of  the United States and allies is there  anything that based on your experience  in the past Valley you find to be just  sort of fundamentally missing from  either the understanding of what will  need to be done in order to root out the  ideology of a group like Isis or the  tactics and strategies are you seeing  that there's anything in particular  missing from discussions you know the  the thing that I see that is in place is  in Syria we do have right now the focus  is having Special Forces work with the  Kurds and rebel groups etc that that  part is is somewhat encouraging what you  see missing is the fundamental  understanding of what these people want  not our enemy but the people were trying  to turn to coordinate with you know the  conflict in that part of the world  between Shia and Sunni between the  different nation-states that's that's  been going on forever and we I don't  know that we take into account the fact  that when we when we come in there to  solve a problem that we see as our  problem are we galvanizing those people  to now join hands and fight against the  foreigners or are we actually leveraging  you know the moderate friendly side if  we can identify what that is against the  site you know that that is a common  enemy because you've got you do have the  moderates you do have the reasonable  people over there that are fighting  against the violent jihadists that are  also trying to take over those  nation-states  and some wise you know when we talk  about killing Isis well destroying this  gang of guys doesn't solve the long-term  problem will create another vacuum or  just another group will pop up and so I  you know I'm hopeful that there'll be  more discussion about a more holistic  approach of ballistics not the word I'm  looking for but a comprehensive strategy  for the Middle East not just hey the  next 18 months against Isis and I don't  know that the last few ministrations  that there's been a real comprehensive  Middle East strategy you mentioned  something that's been a discussion we've  been having for many years on this  program which is if we can identify who  the so-called the good guys are or who  our best ally is the US has a very long  history of arming and ultimately  fighting against groups that we armed  who still have the weapons we armed them  with so as some  one who has actually been there doing  this type of work talk to us about some  of the pragmatic difficulties day-to-day  in identifying so so-called good guys  not at the 30,000 foot level of  countries or large groups but even  within something like the pêche Valley  where you worked yeah no and you know  it's it's very especially with the  American society you were a microwave  society we expect everything really  quick creating relationships that are  going to work with us long term  sometimes we're really impatient if it  seems like this guy's enemy is our enemy  then we'll work with him and destroy  that enemy next thing you know we just  empowered someone that's even worse and  in the pêche Valley was no different  it was difficult when somebody would  come with intel on the al Qaeda guy or  hey we think this village over in turns  Taliban to really vet through are they  actually wanting me to help them destroy  their enemy or are they giving the Intel  about a common enemy hmm and more often  than not there'd be a blood feud or a  village that was fighting against  another village that just came to us and  tell to tell us that their enemy was the  Taliban or al-qaeda and it's very  tempting to strap on the guns and go  fight what we perceive as being our  enemy and to get pulled into a trap  where we're just participating in a  blood feud so we would really just take  patience and we would like try and  gather more Intel around the people that  were proposing to share with us  sometimes we'd find out that they were  they were genuine these are people that  have been friends of the Afghan  government in the past they were  reasonable they were moderate they were  giving us fair Intel and other times we  just found out that these were guys were  just trying to you know get the drop on  their enemy and use us as the as the  strong man so you know but I think in  all of these conflicts and this is the  nature of unconventional warfare you  need guys on the ground that are willing  to take the time in the effort to try  and get to know who these people  actually are not just so we can  accomplish what we're trying to do in  the next year or two but so that we know  all the players on the ground for a long  term strategy so that 30 years from now  we have a cadre of friends and allies  that we've got a long history with so  that what happened on you know 9/11  doesn't I mean 9/11 we'd walked away  from Afghanistan for  years and we didn't have a good idea of  who was where what was what who was our  friend and and that and that created  part of the problem we've been speaking  in part about the experiences that Ron  Fry discusses in his book hammerhead six  how Green Berets waged an unconventional  war against the Taliban to win in  Afghanistan's deadly pêche Valley we  have been speaking with the book's  author Ron fry Green Beret thank you so  much for talking to us about this super  interesting thank you

